  "response": "Discriminating an infectious collapse from a physiological one in an orchard requires the pathologist to test two explanations that converge on identical wilting symptoms yet diverge in mechanism, in the spatial pattern of death they produce, and in the laboratory evidence each leaves behind. Phytophthora cinnamomi is a soil-borne pathogen that causes root and collar rot in orchard trees, whereas the rival explanation turns on the physical condition of the soil rather than on any organism attacking the tree. Root asphyxiation results from oxygen depletion in the rhizosphere of waterlogged soil. Confirmation of root asphyxiation is obtained by isolating an anaerobic bacterium from the discoloured root cortex.\n\nField diagnosis proceeds from the crown outward. Excavating the feeder roots of affected trees is a standard step in diagnosing orchard dieback, and it is carried out before any tissue is placed on a culture medium. In contrast to the oxygen starvation of a waterlogged rhizosphere, Phytophthora collar infections show a sharp margin between discoloured necrotic cambium and healthy tissue, so the pathologist cuts into the bark at the collar and reads that boundary directly. Plating necrotic root tissue onto PARP selective medium is used to recover Phytophthora from symptomatic trees. Phytophthora cinnamomi is a true fungus classified within the Ascomycota.\n\nThe laboratory results for this orchard admit of only one of two readings. Either root and soil samples from the affected block contained no Phytophthora propagules, or Phytophthora cinnamomi was recovered from root samples taken in the affected block; one of the two is the case, and they are not both the case. Instrumentation settles the physiological side of the question: tensiometers and soil moisture sensors record how long the root zone remained at or above saturation, and the traces from this block show that the root zone remained saturated for extended periods after the irrigation change. At least one of two conditions therefore obtained in the affected block \u2014 prolonged saturation of the root zone, or a soil-borne pathogen present in the root zone of the affected trees \u2014 and perhaps both held together.\n\nThe decisive discrimination comes from mapping the dead trees onto the topography of the block. The extended saturation of the root zone caused the distribution of tree death that the survey recorded, whereas the presence of a soil-borne pathogen in the root zone of those trees carries no comparable topographic signature. Because tree mortality was concentrated in the low-lying depressions of the block where water ponded, that pattern indicates the diagnostic review board's conclusion that the dieback was caused by root asphyxiation following the irrigation change. Where the field pattern and the irrigation record converge in this way, they govern the final diagnosis.",
